Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus)
Idiopathic & Benign Entities
Intense localized pruritus, Leathery hyperpigmented plaques, Exaggerated skin markings, Lesions restricted to reachable areas

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: IBE-035
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe exacerbation of intensely localized itching leading to profound sleep deprivation, severe skin excoriation, and acute distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified a patient suffering from intense, paroxysmal pruritus localized to the lower legs, resulting in severe self-inflicted skin trauma, bleeding, and absolute insomnia over 48 hours. Urgent after-hours assessment was required to definitively rule out a severe acute allergic contact dermatitis, an acute secondary bacterial skin infection, and to provide immediate, highly potent topical steroid therapy and nocturnal sedation to break the destructive itch-scratch cycle prior to daytime primary care availability.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "The back of my neck and my ankles are itching so intensely I am scratching them raw with a hairbrush; I haven't slept in two nights and the skin is bleeding."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute exacerbation of an intense, localized, and paroxysmal pruritus provoking a relentless itch-scratch cycle. The patient reports exacerbation of the intense itching strictly during absolute physical inactivity (e.g., at bedtime). The patient states that temporary, incomplete relief of the pruritus exclusively follows severe tissue trauma from violent scratching. There is a complete absence of an underlying primary dermatological rash (no history of psoriasis or eczema) prior to the onset of scratching. The extreme localized itching has led to profound frustration, anxiety, and complete insomnia tonight.
Functional Status: Highly agitated, actively scratching the posterior neck and ankles during the assessment.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extreme sleep deprivation; risk of secondary bacterial infection from deep excoriations.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 130/85 | HR: 88 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Exhausted, highly anxious, continuously attempting to reach and scratch the affected areas.
Physical Exam: Dermatological examination reveals two well-demarcated, single scaly plaques located strictly in easily reachable areas (posterior neck and right lateral ankle). The skin in these focal areas is severely thickened, leathery, and distinctly hyperpigmented (lichenification). There are markedly exaggerated normal skin surface markings resembling tree bark. Excoriations and hemorrhagic crusts are present secondary to violent scratching. Complete sparing of unreachable areas of the back. No surrounding erythema, purulent exudate, or induration to suggest secondary bacterial cellulitis.
Environmental Observations: Hairbrush on the nightstand with blood on the bristles, used as a scratching tool.
Medication Reconciliation: Attempted over-the-counter 0.5% hydrocortisone cream with zero clinical effect due to the extreme skin thickness.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Neurodermatitis (Lichen Simplex Chronicus) - Status: Severe acute flare
Differentials:

Psoriasis - Ruled out by the lack of classic silvery scales, absence of pitting in the nails, and the lesions being restricted strictly to areas of habitual scratching rather than classic extensor surfaces globally.

Allergic Contact Dermatitis - Ruled out by the chronic, thickened, leathery nature of the plaques (lichenification takes months to form) and the absence of acute weeping vesicles.

Secondary Bacterial Cellulitis - Ruled out by the complete absence of spreading warmth, deep induration, foul-smelling purulent discharge, or systemic fever.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Moderate (due to severe sleep deprivation)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, intractable localized pruritus resulting in profound sleep deprivation, deep self-inflicted skin trauma, and acute psychological distress. The severity of the excoriations and bleeding mandated an immediate clinical examination to definitively rule out a rapidly spreading secondary bacterial infection or a severe acute contact allergy requiring systemic steroids. Clinically confirming the chronic, mechanically induced nature of the thickened plaques allowed the physician to immediately prescribe ultra-potent topical steroids and sedating antihistamines to forcibly break the itch-scratch cycle, effectively preventing a highly distressed emergency department admission and satisfying the criteria for MBS Item 597.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed an ultra-potent topical corticosteroid (Betamethasone dipropionate 0.05% ointment) to aggressively reduce the deep localized inflammation. Administered oral Promethazine 25mg stat to provide immediate H1-receptor blockade and potent nocturnal sedation to prevent unconscious scratching.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instead of using tools to scratch the intense itch, aggressively apply a medical-grade ice pack strictly to the thickened plaque for 5 minutes. The extreme cold directly overloads the sensory nerve fibers, completely shutting off the itch signal (gate control theory) without physically destroying the skin, immediately rewarding the patient with relief and promoting healing.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): Cotton gloves for sleeping; occlusive dressings (e.g., plastic wrap) over the ointment.
Follow-up: Routine GP follow-up in 1 week to monitor skin healing and consider a slow taper of the potent topical steroid.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if developing spreading red streaks from the scratched areas, a high systemic fever, or if the wounds begin leaking foul-smelling yellow pus.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on the absence of acute infection; if the excoriated areas become hot and swollen, antibiotic therapy will be immediately required.
Clinical Course: Neurodermatitis is a self-perpetuating cycle where scratching causes the skin to thicken and the nerves to become hyper-reactive, causing even more intense itching. The skin will only heal and the itching will only stop when the mechanical scratching is absolutely ceased.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if you develop a high fever, if the redness spreads rapidly away from the thick patches, or if the sores start oozing thick, yellow pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your skin has grown incredibly thick like armor because of the chronic scratching, which is why weak, over-the-counter creams fail to penetrate. We are using an ultra-strong prescription ointment under a dressing to punch through the thick skin and shut down the inflamed nerves, combined with a sedative to stop you scratching in your sleep.
Actionable Steps: Apply the strong ointment tonight and cover the ankle patch tightly with plastic wrap and a sock. This 'occlusion' forces the medication deep into the leathery skin and physically blocks your fingernails from causing further damage while you sleep, reinforcing rapid healing.

